==== How to Fly: The Long Haul

The 777F day, step by step. A long-haul freighter flight is won on the ground and flown at night: the briefing, the program, the ocean, and the arrival. The aircraft's manual has the exact numbers; this chapter teaches the rhythm, and the rhythm of a fifteen-hour day is what makes the heaviest airplane in the guide feel like the most restful one to fly.

===== The Briefing Ritual

The long-haul briefing is where the flight is decided, and it is a ritual because everything after it is execution. Start with the payload: the cargo, its weight, its position, and the center of gravity it produces — the 777F flies a spreadsheet, and the spreadsheet decides the performance. Then the fuel: trip fuel, reserves, alternate fuel, and contingency, built as a legal document rather than a guess. Then the weather at both ends, and the minimums at the far one — the place where the day can go sideways is known before departure. Then the plan: the route, the oceanic track if there is one, and the diversion plan. The whole night is decided here, calmly, on the ground, so that in the air there is nothing left to decide that has not already been decided well.

===== The FMC Program

With the briefing done, the boxes get the plan. Load the route — the same plan LittleNavMap built during the briefing — through the waypoints, the oceanic entry and exit, the arrival, and the approach. Enter the performance: the weights and fuel into the performance pages, and the box computes the V-speeds and the thrust settings for the whole day. Then cross-check, twice: the LEGS page against the briefing, leg by leg, because a long-haul mistake is a *long* mistake, and the box gets no free pass. Set the automation for the day — the auto-throttle armed, the targets set, the approach armed for the far end — and the aircraft is ready for a flight that will outlast your entire previous career in hours.

===== The Oceanic Track

The ocean is crossed on a track: the organized route over the water, with its entry fix, its exit fix, and its reporting points, loaded and checked before entry. Report position on the schedule the track demands — over an ocean, the position report is the radar, and the track only works if everyone on it plays by the clock. At the halfway and at the exit, check the fuel: burn against plan, and the diversion options re-verified, because the fuel page is the cockpit's most important screen at night over water. And carry the drift-down plan in your head and in the fuel: if an engine stops mid-ocean, the aircraft settles to a single-engine altitude, and the plan for that was made on the ground and loaded into the fuel. It is planning, not pessimism — it is what lets you enjoy the quiet.

===== The Night Cruise

The quiet hours are the real work. The autopilot flies, the fuel burns, and the crew manages the systems and the plan: hourly checks of the fuel page, the position, and the weather picture, with the rhythm of the crew day — rest in rotation, sharpness saved for the arrival. The sim may not need rest, but the habit of managing the day does, and the habit is what the airline version of you would thank you for. As the hours pass, the top of descent arrives, computed and confirmed: the arrival begins an hour before the airport appears, with energy, weather, and the approach all starting to come together. The long night ends the way it began — with the plan.

===== The Minimums Arrival

The far end of the day is often weather, and the arrival is the same briefing as every wave, at the end of a very long day: the setup, the path, the minimums, the missed — briefed from the plate in AviTab while the aircraft descends. In low visibility, the automation flies the approach to very low minimums and the crew monitors like hawks: needles, speed, mode annunciations, because the automation is powerful and the monitoring is the job. At the decision altitude, the call: the runway environment in sight, or the missed — the missed is the procedure you briefed twelve hours ago, and flying it is not a disappointment, it is the plan. And when the wheels are down and the reverse is in, the dock: the cargo doors, the payload off, and the night's promise kept. The freighter's day ends with the cargo where it was promised, which is the only scoreboard that matters.

===== The Debrief

Every long-haul flight deserves a debrief, and the long-haul debrief is a numbers conversation: fuel burn against plan, time against plan, every decimal a lesson about the wind, the weight, or the planning. Then the decisions: the diversions considered, the minimums respected — the log of judgment, not just of flight time. And the logbook itself: six waves of entries, from the first circuit at Cedar Harbor to the transatlantic night. The ace's logbook is the point of Ace's Realm — every entry a flight that was planned, flown, and reviewed, and every review a little more of the calm that the heavy iron demands.
